Predict the experimental log human liver microsomal clearance value (as log10 of the value in mL/min/kg) for the molecule shown: O=C(CSc1ccc2c(c1)OCCO2)NC1CCCC1.
The protocol of the experimental assay is as follows: Compounds at 1~$\mu$ M (final concentration) were incubated with human liver microsomes (0.5 mg/mL) in 0.1 M sodium phosphate buffer (pH 7.4) plus 3.3 mM magnesium chloride in the presence of 1 mM nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate (NADPH) at 37~$^{\circ}$C; aliquots (40~$\mu$ L) at 0, 5, 10, 15, 25, and 40 min post NADPH addition were transferred into individual 96-well plate wells containing 50 ng/mL of 8-cyclopently-1,3-dipropylxanthine (CPDPX, internal standard) in 200~$\mu$ L of acetonitrile:methanol (1:1 v/v), centrifuged (10 min, 3220g), and 40~$\mu$ L of supernatant from each well was transferred into a clean analytical sample 96-well plate well containing 180~$\mu$ L of water:formic acid (100:0.1 v/v); these samples were mixed and directly injected onto a LC/MS/MS system for sample analysis; CLint (intrinsic clearance) was calculated using the equation for microsome stability:
\begin{align*}
\text{CL}_{\text{int}} &= \frac{0.693}{T_{1/2}} \times \frac{\text{incubation volume}}{\text{mg of microsomal protein}}
\end{align*}
CLscaled (scaled intrinsic clearance) was calculated using:
\begin{align*}
\text{CL}_{\text{scaled}} &= \frac{0.693}{T_{1/2}} \times \frac{\text{incubation volume}}{\text{mg of microsomal protein}} \times \frac{45 \text{ mg of microsomal protein}}{\text{grams of liver}} \times \frac{20^{a} \text{ grams of liver}}{\text{kg of body weight}}
\end{align*}
where “a” represents 20 g of liver/kg of body weight for human.

Conclude your response with: Correct Answer >> floating_point_number. The final result should be a single floating point number.

